<body>
{% macro panel(key, title) %}
<figure class="panel">
  <p class="title">{{ title }}</p>
  {{ fig_svg(key, title) }}
  {% set src = caller() %}{% if src | trim %}<p class="source">{{ src }}</p>{% endif %}
  {{ tables.get(key, "") }}
</figure>
{% endmacro %}

{% macro numbers(section, summary) %}
<details class="numbers" id="{{ section }}-numbers">
  <summary><span>All the numbers<small>{{ summary }}</small></span></summary>
  <div class="numbers-body">
    {{ caller() }}
    <h3>Sources</h3>
    {{ n.section_list() }}
  </div>
</details>
{% endmacro %}

<header class="wrap hero">
  <h1>Garbage in,<br> <span class="out">guilt out.<svg class="swoosh" viewBox="0 0 300 30" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3,20 C70,6 170,2 297,13"/></svg></span></h1>
  <a class="cue" href="#ocean" aria-label="Start reading"><span>&darr;</span></a>
</header>

<main>
{{ n.start("ocean") }}
<section class="round r-ocean" id="ocean">
<div class="wrap">
  <p class="spine"><span class="n">01</span>Almost none of it reaches the ocean.</p>
  <div class="punch" id="c1">
    <div>
      <p class="punch-num">26x.</p>
      <p class="punch-line">One river in Manila puts 26 times more plastic into the ocean than every US river combined.{{ n.fn("meijer_data", "owid_meijer") }}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="punch-art">{{ figs.c1 }}</div>
  </div>

  <div class="beat">
    {% call panel("o3", "The US is a quarter of one percent of the plastic rivers carry to the ocean") %}Source: {{ n.cite("owid_meijer", "Meijer et al. 2021, Science Advances") }}, 2015 estimates{% endcall %}
    <p class="callout">US rivers carry about 2,400 tonnes of plastic to the ocean a year: 0.25% of the world total, about 7 grams per American.{{ n.fn("owid_meijer", "calc:ocean") }}</p>
    <p class="explain">The river model leaves out US plastic shipped abroad. Even counted at worst case, the US stays under 2% of the world total, and the Pasig alone is still nearly 4 times the whole US.{{ n.fn("rr2025", "owid_trade", "calc:ocean") }} The 26x figure above counts US rivers only.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="beat">
    {% call panel("o4", "An American's yearly 7 g is less than one 8 g grocery bag") %}Sources: {{ n.cite("owid_meijer", "Meijer et al. 2021") }}; bag weight from {{ n.cite("uk_ea_bags", "UK Environment Agency") }}{% endcall %}
    <div class="qa">
      <p class="q">What would help the ocean?</p>
      <p class="callout">Waste collection where it is missing.</p>
      <p class="explain">Mismanaged plastic per person in the Philippines is 84 times the UK's.{{ n.fn("owid_mismanaged") }}</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  {% call numbers("ocean", "Exports, the factsheet, methods and sources") %}
    <h3>Exports</h3>
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th>Stat</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>US plastic scrap exports, 2024: 903M lb (about 410,000 tonnes)</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("rr2025", "Resource Recycling") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>55% went to Canada and Mexico; 28% to India, Malaysia, Vietnam and Indonesia</td><td class="src">Same; shares calculated</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Method: treat every tonne sent to Mexico and those four countries (about 202,000 t) as mismanaged, with 1% to 7% reaching the sea</td><td class="src">Bounds from {{ n.cite("owid_trade", "Our World in Data") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Result: exports add 2,000 to 14,100 t, so the US total is 4,400 to 16,600 t a year, 0.45% to 1.7% of the world's</td><td class="src">Calculation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The Pasig River alone is still 3.8 to 14 times the US total, exports included</td><td class="src">Calculation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Context: world plastic waste trade fell about two-thirds after 2010, and China stopped taking it in 2018</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("owid_trade", "Our World in Data") }}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3>Factsheet</h3>
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th>Stat</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>US: 2,431 tonnes a year enter the ocean from rivers (2015)</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("meijer", "Meijer et al. 2021, <em>Science Advances</em>"|safe) }}, country data</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Per American: about 7 g a year, less than one 8 g grocery bag</td><td class="src">2,431 t / 328M people (2019); bag weight from {{ n.cite("uk_ea_bags", "UK Environment Agency") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Per person: Philippines 3.2 kg a year; Europe under 0.1 kg</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("owid_meijer", "Meijer et al. 2021") }} country data, per person (2019 population)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Asia: 81% of global inputs</td><td class="src">Sum of Asian countries in {{ n.cite("owid_meijer", "Meijer et al. 2021") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Marine sources (fishing gear, ropes, abandoned vessels): 20 to 30% of ocean plastic by weight. Lines, ropes and nets are 52% of the plastic mass in the Great Pacific Garbage Patch</td><td class="src">Patch: {{ n.cite("lebreton", "Lebreton et al. 2018") }}, <em>Scientific Reports</em>. 20 to 30% range: {{ n.cite("owid_ocean", "Our World in Data") }}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3 id="ocean-methods">How we calculated</h3>
    <p>A model of plastic over 0.5 cm reaching the sea through rivers (Meijer et al. 2021).{{ n.fn("meijer") }} The paper puts each river's estimate within a factor of 4 (68% confidence), so a river estimated at 100 tonnes could plausibly be 25 to 400. It excludes waste shipped abroad and microplastics.</p>
    <h3>Common objections</h3>
    <div class="objection">
      <h4>What would help the ocean?</h4>
      <p>Waste collection where it is missing. Mismanaged plastic per person in the Philippines is 84 times the UK's ({{ n.cite("owid_mismanaged", "Meijer et al. 2021") }} data).</p>
    </div>
  {% endcall %}
</div>
</section>

{{ n.start("landfills") }}
<section class="round r-landfills" id="landfills">
<div class="wrap">
  <p class="spine"><span class="n">02</span>What gets buried takes up almost no room.</p>
  <div class="punch" id="c2">
    <div>
      <p class="punch-num long">Twin bed.</p>
      <p class="punch-line">Your lifetime of trash, buried, takes up less floor space than a twin bed.{{ n.fn("epa2018", "calc:landfills") }}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="punch-art">{{ figs.l3 }}</div>
  </div>

  <div class="beat">
    <p class="callout">One American's lifetime of trash, buried 100 ft deep, covers about 15 sq ft: less than a twin bed.{{ n.fn("epa2018", "lmop_data", "calc:landfills") }}</p>
    <p class="explain">Burned like Japan, it shrinks to about 2.5 sq ft, under a 2 x 3 ft doormat.{{ n.fn("japan_moe") }} A full year of US landfilled trash fits on Golden Gate Park (1,017 acres), piled about 144 ft high.{{ n.fn("ggp", "calc:landfills") }}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="beat">
    {% call panel("l4", "Modern landfills are sealed, drained and monitored.") %}{% endcall %}
    <p class="callout">New York reviewed 31 lined landfills built after 1988 and found no groundwater harm from leaks through their liners.{{ n.fn("nrc2007") }}</p>
    <p class="explain">About 58% of landfill methane comes from food waste; plastic does not rot. The fix is food scraps, not plastic.{{ n.fn("epa_food_methane") }}</p>
  </div>

  {% call numbers("landfills", "The factsheet, methods, objections and sources") %}
    <h3>Factsheet</h3>
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th>Stat</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>US, 2018: 292.4M tons generated; 146.1M landfilled (50%); 34.6M burned for energy (11.8%)</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("epa2018", "EPA") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Per American: about 890 lb landfilled a year, about 19 cu ft</td><td class="src"><a href="#landfills-methods">Calculation</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Per lifetime (79 years): about 1,540 cu ft, less than one one-car garage (12 x 20 x 8 ft)</td><td class="src"><a href="#landfills-methods">Calculation</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Footprint at 100 ft deep: about 15 sq ft; a twin bed is 20 sq ft (38 x 75 in)</td><td class="src"><a href="#landfills-methods">Calculation</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Burned like Japan, it shrinks to about 2.5 sq ft, under a 2 x 3 ft doormat. Japan sends 8.0% of its waste to final disposal (2024); the US landfills 50%. Safety of burning: <a href="#burning">see below</a></td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("japan_moe", "Japan Ministry of the Environment") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A full year of US landfilled trash fits on Golden Gate Park (1,017 acres), piled about 144 ft high</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("ggp", "SF Recreation and Parks") }}; calculation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Landfills cover about 0.01% of US land</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("lmop_data", "EPA landfill data") }}, via {{ n.cite("wip", "Works in Progress") }}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3 id="landfills-methods">How we calculated</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Density: 0.62 short tons per cu yd, the compacted density in {{ n.cite("pa_landfill", "one Pennsylvania landfill's filing") }}; EPA landfill data put the median near 0.64. At the 1997 national average of 0.37 ({{ n.cite("waste360_2011", "739 lb per cu yd") }}), the footprint would be 26 sq ft.</li>
      <li>Depth: 100 ft is the median design depth of open US landfills in {{ n.cite("lmop_data", "EPA's landfill data") }}. Life: 79 years, US life expectancy in 2024 ({{ n.cite("cdc_life", "CDC") }}).</li>
      <li>Japan scaling uses tonnage (6.2x less). Ash is denser than raw trash, so the real volume is smaller.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Common objections</h3>
    <div class="objection">
      <h4>Don't landfills leak?</h4>
      <p>New York reviewed 31 lined landfills built after 1988 (27 municipal, 4 hazardous waste), about 450 years of combined operation, and found no groundwater harm from leaks through their liners ({{ n.cite("nrc2007", "National Academies") }}).</p>
      <h4>What about methane?</h4>
      <p>Municipal landfills produce 14.4% of US methane ({{ n.cite("epa_lmop", "EPA, 2022") }}). About 58% of it comes from food waste ({{ n.cite("epa_food_methane", "EPA") }}); plastic does not rot. Aerial surveys found landfills emit 1.4 to 2.7 times the methane they report to EPA ({{ n.cite("cusworth", "Cusworth et al. 2024") }}). The fix is food scraps, not plastic.</p>
      <h4>Are we running out of space?</h4>
      <p>The US has about 62 years of capacity in its current landfills, by a 2015 EREF estimate ({{ n.cite("waste360_capacity", "Waste360") }}). Shortages are regional and come from permitting, not a lack of land.</p>
      <h4 id="burning">Isn't burning trash toxic?</h4>
      <p>Not with modern controls. From 1990 to 2005, US waste combustors cut dioxin emissions by over 99% (4,400 to 15 g TEQ a year), mercury by 96% and lead by 97% ({{ n.cite("epa_combustion", "EPA") }}). EPA says fossil-fuel power plants produce most US mercury and particulate pollution (same source, 2005 data). Copenhagen's CopenHill plant burned 610,000 t of waste in 2024, supplied electricity for about 99,000 homes and heat for about 92,000, and has a public ski slope on its roof ({{ n.cite("arc2024", "ARC annual report 2024") }}). Its climate cost is already counted in the climate section: burning is the 3.08 kg CO2 per kg line.</p>
    </div>
  {% endcall %}
</div>
</section>

{{ n.start("climate") }}
<section class="round r-climate" id="climate">
<div class="wrap">
  <p class="spine"><span class="n">03</span>Your plastic's climate cost is small.</p>
  <div class="punch" id="c3">
    <div>
      <p class="punch-num">3x.</p>
      <p class="punch-line">Your dog has 3 times the climate footprint of your plastic.{{ n.fn("berners_lee", "calc:climate") }} You don't sweat the kibble. Don't sweat the bag.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="punch-art">{{ figs.c3 }}</div>
  </div>

  <div class="beat">
    {% call panel("k4", "A year of your plastic, 130 to 250 kg of CO2e, is the smallest line here") %}Sources: {{ n.cite("berners_lee", "Berners-Lee") }}; {{ n.cite("edinburgh", "Edinburgh") }}; {{ n.cite("ers_cheese", "USDA ERS") }}; {{ n.cite("poore", "Poore and Nemecek 2018") }}; {{ n.cite("shaka", "Shaka Guide") }}; {{ n.cite("epa_vehicle", "EPA") }}; {{ n.cite("desnz", "DESNZ") }}; <a href="#climate-methods">how we calculated</a>{% endcall %}
    <p class="callout">A year of one American's single-use plastic emits about 130 to 250 kg of CO2e, roughly 0.7 to 1.3% of a typical US footprint.{{ n.fn("warm_plastics", "epa_ghg", "calc:climate") }}</p>
    <p class="explain">An American throws away about 53 kg of single-use plastic a year.{{ n.fn("minderoo") }} So: 130 kg a year if all buried, 250 kg if all burned.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="beat">
    <p class="callout">Pound for pound, plastic is gasoline.</p>
    <p class="explain">Burning a pound of polyethylene emits the same CO2 as burning a pound of gasoline, 3.08 vs 3.07 kg per kg.{{ n.fn("warm_plastics", "ipcc2006") }} A year of your plastic, 130 to 250 kg CO2e, equals 15 to 28 gallons of gas at 8.887 kg per gallon: one or one and a half drives to Tahoe and back.{{ n.fn("epa_vehicle") }} Rule of thumb: weigh your plastic, and picture that weight in gasoline.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="beat">
    <div class="panel">
      <p class="title">Uses needed to match one plastic grocery bag</p>
      <table class="mini">
        <thead><tr><th>Bag</th><th class="n">Climate only</th><th class="n">All environmental impacts</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Paper</td><td class="n">0 to 1</td><td class="n">43</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Woven plastic tote</td><td class="n">5</td><td class="n">45</td></tr>
          <tr class="hot"><td>Cotton tote</td><td class="n">52</td><td class="n">7,100</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Organic cotton tote</td><td class="n">149</td><td class="n">20,000</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="source">Baseline: a standard 24 g supermarket plastic bag, reused once as a bin liner. Cotton's all-category figure is driven by ozone depletion; without it, 50 to 1,400 uses (organic cotton: 150 to 3,800). Source: {{ n.cite("danish_epa", "Danish Environmental Protection Agency 2018, LCA of grocery carrier bags, Table IV") }}.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  {% call numbers("climate", "The ledger, methods, objections and sources") %}
    <h3>The ledger</h3>
    <p>Per kg of plastic: making it, +2.5 kg CO2e; burning it, +3.08; electricity recovered from burning, minus 0.86; net 4.7 kg CO2e if burned, 2.5 kg if buried.{{ n.fn("warm_plastics", "zheng_suh") }} An American throws away about 53 kg of single-use plastic a year.{{ n.fn("minderoo") }} So: 130 kg a year if all buried, 250 kg if all burned. Every input is sourced below; polyethylene values are used throughout.</p>
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Figure</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Make: resin</td><td>1.68 kg CO2e per kg (virgin HDPE, 1.52 t per short ton)</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("warm_plastics", "EPA WARM v16, Plastics, Exhibit 5-7") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Make: forming it into products</td><td>Adds 49% on top of resin (global conversion 535 Mt vs resin 1,085 Mt CO2e, 2015). 1.68 x 1.49 = 2.5</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("zheng_suh", "Zheng and Suh 2019") }}, <em>Nature Climate Change</em></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Burn</td><td>3.08 kg CO2 per kg (86% carbon, 98% oxidized)</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("warm_plastics", "EPA WARM v16, Exhibit 5-17") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Energy back</td><td>2.3 kWh per kg (40.0 MMBtu per short ton at 17.8% plant efficiency) x 0.373 kg CO2 per kWh = 0.86</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("warm_plastics", "EPA WARM v16, Exhibit 5-18") }}; grid factor 823.1 lb per MWh, {{ n.cite("egrid", "EPA eGRID2022") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bury</td><td>0.02 kg per kg (trucks only; plastic makes no methane)</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("warm_plastics", "EPA WARM v16, Exhibit 5-19") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Use</td><td>53 kg single-use plastic waste per American, 2019</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("minderoo", "Minderoo Foundation, Plastic Waste Makers Index 2021") }}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <figure>
      <p class="title">Burned, a kg of plastic nets 4.7 kg of CO2e; buried, 2.5 kg</p>
      {{ fig_svg("k3", "Burned, a kg of plastic nets 4.7 kg of CO2e; buried, 2.5 kg") }}
      <p class="source">Source: {{ n.cite("warm_plastics", "EPA WARM v16") }}; {{ n.cite("zheng_suh", "Zheng and Suh 2019") }}; {{ n.cite("egrid", "EPA eGRID2022") }}</p>
      {{ tables.k3 }}
    </figure>
    <h3>Pound for pound, plastic is gasoline</h3>
    <p>Burning a pound of polyethylene emits the same CO2 as burning a pound of gasoline, 3.08 vs 3.07 kg per kg ({{ n.cite("warm_plastics", "EPA WARM v16, Exhibit 5-17") }}; {{ n.cite("ipcc2006", "IPCC 2006 defaults") }}: 69,300 kg CO2 per TJ x 44.3 TJ per thousand t). Counting manufacture, a pound of plastic is 0.8x (buried) to 1.5x (burned) a pound of gasoline at the tailpipe.</p>
    <h3 id="climate-methods">How we calculated</h3>
    <p>Greenhouse gases in CO2e on both sides; the burning, driving and flight lines are almost all CO2.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Flight: 8,630 km each way x 0.069 kg per passenger-km, the UK government's 2025 long-haul economy factor without contrail effects ({{ n.cite("desnz", "DESNZ") }}).</li>
      <li>Tahoe: 190 to 220 miles each way ({{ n.cite("shaka", "Shaka Guide") }}) x 0.4 kg per mile ({{ n.cite("epa_vehicle", "EPA") }}) x 5 trips.</li>
      <li>Dog: 770 kg for an average-sized dog ({{ n.cite("berners_lee", "Mike Berners-Lee") }}) and 530 kg for its food alone, with a different way of splitting meat emissions between pet and human food ({{ n.cite("edinburgh", "Edinburgh") }}).</li>
      <li>Cheese: 40.15 lb per person in 2023 ({{ n.cite("ers_cheese", "USDA ERS") }}) x 23.88 kg CO2e per kg ({{ n.cite("poore", "Poore and Nemecek 2018") }}, <em>Science</em>).</li>
      <li>US footprint: 18,990 kg CO2e per person, gross emissions in 2022 ({{ n.cite("epa_ghg", "EPA inventory") }}).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>WARM covers resin only, so the 49% for forming products comes from the global study. Zheng and Suh's global average, about 4.0 kg per kg to make on coal-heavy grids, would raise the burned case to 330 kg; that is still under the dog's low estimate of 530. WARM credits burning against marginal power plants (1.68 per kg); we use the average grid, which is less favorable to burning. On EPA's current marginal rate, the burned case would be 218 kg.</p>
    <p>The card's "3x" uses the dog's high estimate (770 kg) against the burned plastic (250 kg). The chart shows the full ranges.</p>
    <h3>Common objections</h3>
    <div class="objection">
      <h4>Isn't plastic made from oil?</h4>
      <p>Yes. Drilling, refining and manufacturing are all inside the 2.5 kg production figure.</p>
    </div>
  {% endcall %}
</div>
</section>

{{ n.start("bin") }}
<section class="round r-bin" id="bin">
<div class="wrap">
  <p class="spine"><span class="n">04</span>Recycling plastic barely helps.</p>
  <div class="punch">
    <div>
      <p class="punch-num">76%.</p>
      <p class="punch-line">Three quarters of US plastic goes to landfill.{{ n.fn("epa_plastics") }}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="punch-art">{{ figs.r3 }}</div>
  </div>

  <div class="beat">
    {% call panel("r4", "Even PET, the most recycled plastic, is at 18.5%") %}Source: EPA 2018 data, in {{ n.cite("greenpeace2022", "Greenpeace 2022, Table A-3") }}{% endcall %}
    <p class="callout">Under 10% of US plastic is recycled; aluminum cans are the bin item with by far the largest climate payoff per pound.{{ n.fn("epa_plastics", "warm_plastics") }}</p>
    <p class="explain">Recycling mid-grade plastic costs an estimated $1,000 or more per tonne of CO2 avoided; direct air capture sells for about $490 on average.{{ n.fn("wip", "wri_dac") }}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="beat">
    <div class="callout-card">
      {{ figs.can }}
      <div>
        <p class="callout">The one thing worth rinsing: cans.</p>
        <p class="explain">About 0.13 kg of CO2e saved per can, so roughly 1,900 cans equal a year of single-use plastic.{{ n.fn("warm_plastics", "alu_lca", "calc:climate") }}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="beat qa">
    <p class="q">So should I stop recycling?</p>
    <p class="answer">For plastic, yes.</p>
    <p class="explain">About three quarters of it is landfilled anyway, recycling it costs an estimated $900 or more per ton of CO2 avoided, and a year of it is under 2% of your footprint. Keep rinsing cans: each one saves about 0.3 lb of CO2e.{{ n.fn("epa_plastics", "wip", "calc:climate", "warm_plastics") }}</p>
  </div>

  {% call numbers("bin", "The factsheet and sources") %}
    <h3>Factsheet</h3>
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th>Stat</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>US plastic, 2018: 35.7M tons; 3.1M recycled (8.7%), 5.6M burned for energy, 27M landfilled</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("epa_plastics", "EPA, Plastics: Material-Specific Data") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>By 2021 the rate was about 5%, by estimate: 2.4M of 51M tons</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("lbc2022", "Last Beach Cleanup and Beyond Plastics") }}, cited by Greenpeace</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Recycled, 2018: PET (#1) 18.5%, HDPE (#2) 8.9%, types #3 to #7 under 5%</td><td class="src">EPA data, in {{ n.cite("greenpeace2022", "Greenpeace 2022, Table A-3") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>About 17% of what goes into US curbside recycling is contamination</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("trp2020", "The Recycling Partnership") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Recycling mid-grade plastic costs an estimated $1,000 or more per tonne of CO2 avoided; direct air capture sells for about $490 on average</td><td class="src">Estimate: {{ n.cite("wip", "Works in Progress") }}; price: {{ n.cite("wri_dac", "WRI") }}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Recycling a short ton of aluminum cans instead of landfilling saves 9.15 t CO2e, about 0.13 kg per can</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("warm_plastics", "EPA WARM v16, Exhibit 2-7") }}; per-can figure assumes a 13 g can ({{ n.cite("alu_lca", "Aluminum Association") }})</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Recycling programs' own advice: when in doubt, throw it out</td><td class="src">{{ n.cite("epa_doubt", "EPA") }}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    {{ tables.r3 }}
  {% endcall %}
</div>
</section>
</main>

